seinfeld might be one of the funniest  shows in tv history but showrunner larry  david had some truly dark rules about  its storylines keep watching to learn  more about david's bleak rulings  seinfeld was famously a show about  nothing jerry george elaine and kramer  would get into all kinds of awkward  predicaments which is typical for  sitcoms however in most other  programming the characters learn some  sort of lesson about morality and or  ethics they grow into better people by  the end of the episode even if they  completely forget said lesson by the  next one but not seinfeld the characters  would be precisely the same by each  episode's end it turns out there's a  good reason why because show creator  larry david had two very strict rules  about seinfeld no hugging and no  learning you can see this ethos in every  half hour installment there aren't any  sentimental moments where the friends  come together to hug one another after a  misunderstanding and they never learn  their lessons often going through a  comedy of errors just to wind up the  same exact people at episode's end as  the infamous series finale showed their  inability to become better people is the  whole point david once elaborated on  this to an interviewer saying a lot of  people don't understand that seinfeld is  a dark show if you examine the premises  terrible things happen to people they  lose jobs somebody breaks up with a  stroke victim somebody's told they need  a nose job that's my sensibility at  their core the four friends are awful  people as exhibited in season five's the  hamptons when jerry and elaine meet a  friend's new baby they proceed to call  it the ugliest baby that either one of  them has ever seen oh a little too much  chlorine in that gene pool and that's  them actually being nice on multiple  occasions they actually killed someone  by accident look at the insanely dark  season 8 episode the czechs in the  episode jerry hits elaine's boyfriend in  the head with an axe and he dies during  the subsequent surgery when the surgeon  gets distracted amazingly no one learns  a lesson of any kind instead the death  is just played for dark laughs but  nowhere is the show's insistence on  everyone being awful more evident than  in the episode the invitations in which  george's fiance susan dies usually when  a major character on a sitcom dies it's  a very special episode played straight  with some kind of emotional message but  not on seinfeld when susan died it was  done in a classic seinfeld-esque way she  was poisoned by licking cheap envelopes  because george was too stingy to pay for  nicer wedding invitations and george  isn't at all upset by her death in fact  he's not so secretly happy about it  because he had been trying to figure out  a way to break it off without looking  like the bad guy with her death not only  does he get out of the marriage but he  gets sympathy to boot he couldn't be  more pleased he's dead  yes  the reason she was killed off sounds  like it could have come out of a  seinfeld episode itself as jason  alexander later revealed that actress  heidi swedberg was fired from the show  because he couldn't figure out her sense  of humor  he told howard stern her instincts for  doing a scene where the comedy was and  mine were always misfiring and she would  do something and i would go okay i see  what she's going to do i'm going to  adjust her and then i'd adjust and she'd  change which brings us back to that  series finale where the four characters  go to jail when instead of helping a  robbery victim they merely watch and  laugh it was larry david's way of  telling the devoted audience that we  really shouldn't have been laughing at  this stuff all these years because these  are fundamentally bad people we're  identifying with the success of the show  ultimately inspired a whole sub-genre of  popular sitcoms like it's always sunny  in philadelphia and you're the worst  about bad people doing bad things of  course one of those is curve your  enthusiasm larry david's hbo show that  could be considered a spiritual  successor to seinfeld in a lot of ways  nevertheless we keep cheering these  awful protagonists on so it seems that  like the seinfeld characters themselves  we didn't learn anything either check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite shows are coming soon subscribe  to our youtube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

